\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Chiralities are integers and cancel}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{\Sigma} be a \vocabk{closed surface}{fdip-015O} with every triangle regular, on a grid of any
dimension #{n\ge 2}. Let the Hermitian symbol act on a finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space.
At every vertex of #{\Sigma} suppose it is invertible with the same number #{q\ge 1}
of negative eigenvalues. The triangle products and fluxes are independent of the chosen orthonormal bases. Then
#{\operatorname{chir}(\Sigma)} is an integer, and #{\operatorname{chir}(-\Sigma)=-\operatorname{chir}(\Sigma)}.
Now let #{n\ge 3}, and let #{\mathcal Z=\sum_\sigma c_\sigma\sigma} be a combination of oriented
three-dimensional simplices of the grid with integer coefficients and zero boundary. Let #{R_0} be the
set of vertices at which #{\mathcal B} is not invertible, let #{\mathcal Y} be the sum of the terms of
#{\mathcal Z} whose simplex has a vertex in #{R_0}, and put #{\mathcal W=\mathcal Z-\mathcal Y}.
Suppose that every triangle of the boundary #{\partial\sigma} of every simplex of #{\mathcal W} is
regular, with #{q} negative eigenvalues at its vertices. Then
##{\operatorname{chir}(\partial\mathcal Y)=-\sum_{\sigma\in\mathcal W}c_\sigma\operatorname{chir}(\partial\sigma),}
each term being an integer. In particular, if the boundary of every simplex of #{\mathcal W} has chirality #{0},
then #{\operatorname{chir}(\partial\mathcal Y)=0}.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{d_{vw}=\det(V_v^\dagger V_w)} on oriented edges. Changing the
two orthonormal bases by unitaries #{A_v,A_w} multiplies #{d_{vw}} by
#{(\det A_v)^{-1}\det A_w}. These factors cancel around a triangle, so
#{z} and its principal argument are unchanged. On a regular triangle
each edge determinant is nonzero. Divide each by its absolute value to
obtain an edge unit. Reversing the edge conjugates its determinant and
inverts the unit. The product of the three units on #{t} is
#{\ue^{\ui\operatorname{flux}(t)}}.}

\p{Raise each triangle product of units to its signed coefficient #{c_t}
and multiply. The exponent of each oriented edge is its coefficient in
#{\partial\Sigma}, hence zero. Thus #{\sum_t c_t\operatorname{flux}(t)}
is an integral multiple of #{2\pi}. Reversal conjugates the triangle
product, and its principal argument changes sign because the cut is
excluded. This gives integrality and the reversal formula.}

\p{Every edge in the boundary of a simplex boundary occurs twice with
opposite signs. Hence each #{\partial\sigma} is closed. On the simplices
of #{\mathcal W} the regularity and rank hypotheses give an integer
on each such boundary. Since #{\partial\mathcal Z=0},
#{\partial\mathcal Y=-\partial\mathcal W} coefficient by coefficient.
Every triangle surviving on either side occurs in a boundary from
#{\mathcal W}, so it has a defined flux. Summing its angle with
the induced coefficients gives the displayed equality by linearity,
and a sum of zero terms vanishes.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.det_conjTranspose} gives the reversed
edge determinant. Its \leanref{Complex.exp_eq_one_iff} gives the
integer periods of the complex exponential.}
